<ShakespeareLayout
  title="Honoring Our Guides"
  subPage="honoring-our-guides"
  eyebrow="Shakespeare"
  description="Acknowledging the teachers, translators, and institutions whose work shapes DT:FC&rsquo;s Shakespeare section."
>
  <article class="prose max-w-2xl">
    <p>
      We are grateful perpetually for those who came before us as we evolve our training and
      decades of experience into an international Developmental Theatre: Fearless Creativity
      platform.
    </p>

    <section id="shakespeare">
      <h2>William Shakespeare</h2>
      <p>
        The works of William Shakespeare are gifts given to the world. We are privileged to be
        actors, teachers, authors, and others who perform, teach and use the plays centuries after
        his company performed them. None of what we are would exist without the Bard of Avon.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="colorado-shakespeare-festival">
      <h2>The Colorado Shakespeare Festival</h2>
      <p>
        We recognize The Colorado Shakespeare Festival&rsquo;s decades of summer programs which
        provided intensive experience in performing two to three plays for around two months of
        rotating performances.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="the-caravan">
      <h2>The Colorado Caravan</h2>
      <p>
        We acknowledge with gratitude the University of Colorado&rsquo;s role in the Colorado
        Caravan program, originally created to provide winter work touring Shakespeare plays to
        schools and communities in Colorado.
      </p>
      <p>
        Within four years the Colorado Caravan experience fostered the Department&rsquo;s
        Developmental Theatre M.A. program. Many department professors (see{' '}
        <a href="/legacy/founders/">Legacy &rarr; Founders</a>) also directed plays for the
        Colorado Shakespeare Festival. Both founders Richard Knaub and Martin Cobin are examples.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="daniel-yang">
      <h2>Daniel S.P. Yang</h2>
      <p>
        Daniel S.P. Yang taught Asian Theatre to many of the Colorado Caravan student actors, and
        was a supporter of the Colorado Caravan. Asian Theatre is a direct influence. Currently he
        is translating Shakespeare into Chinese.
      </p>
      <p>
        Yang&rsquo;s Asian Theatre influence shaped DT:FC&rsquo;s approach to staging &mdash; see{' '}
        <a href="/legacy/essays/theatre-influences/#asian-theatre">
          the Asian Theatre section in Legacy&rsquo;s Theatre Influences essay
        </a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="chuck-wilcox">
      <h2>Chuck Wilcox</h2>
      <p>
        Chuck Wilcox was hired by the Colorado Shakespeare Festival and helped to create the first
        years of the Colorado Caravan. He played Kent in <em>King Lear</em> his first summer; it
        was directed by Robert Benedetti, whose approach to the production reinforced the blank
        stage, no props, Poor Theatre style the Colorado Caravan used. After a stroke limited
        Chuck&rsquo;s mobility Richard Devin and he invented the Shakespeare on the Green program,
        where Chuck visited theatre patrons as Will Shakespeare.
      </p>
      <p>
        Four years after the closing of the Colorado Caravan and the end of the Developmental
        Theatre M.A. program, Chuck also toured as Will for the Colorado Shakespeare Festival
        educational programs, sponsored by Richard Devin as director of the CSF.
      </p>
      <p>
        See <a href="/legacy/founders/#chuck-wilcox">Chuck&rsquo;s fuller story on the Founders
        page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="melinda-scott">
      <h2>Melinda Scott</h2>
      <p>
        These tours were organized for ten years by Melinda Scott. Melinda performed in Will Power
        with Chuck, as did Marta Barnard who went on to tour her own theatres in the midwest USA.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="marta-barnard">
      <h2>Marta Barnard</h2>
      <p>
        Marta Barnard performed in Will Power with Chuck and Melinda, then went on to tour her own
        theatres in the midwest USA. Marta made many contributions to this website.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="amanda-giguere">
      <h2>Amanda Giguere</h2>
      <p>
        Amanda Giguere has for many years been the CSF educational program leader.
        She published <em>Shakespeare and Violence Prevention: A Practical Handbook for
        Educators</em> in June, 2025.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="joe-craft">
      <h2>Joe Craft</h2>
      <p>
        We would like to honor Joe Craft who taught Drama and Speech for fifty years in Denver CO
        public schools. He founded the Denver Public Schools Shakespeare Festival, which continues
        today. When it was selected as a national demonstration site, Joe was appointed as the
        third steward of the Folger Shakespeare Library in Washington, DC. Joe&rsquo;s
        vision of Shakespeare transformed many young lives, and is part of the fabric of DT:FC.
      </p>
    </section>

    <footer>
      <p>
        Many of these guides also appear in the fuller DT:FC lineage &mdash; see{' '}
        <a href="/legacy/founders">Legacy &rarr; Founders</a> for the wider story.
      </p>
    </footer>
  </article>
</ShakespeareLayout>
